WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.280
What’s up, guys?

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.080
Jared here.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:11.579
Unless you’re brand new to the internet
you’ve probably encountered some good old

00:00:11.579 --> 00:00:13.889
fashioned online rage.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:19.609
Like grief at a funeral, happiness at a pizza
party, or humiliation at a bar mitzvah, rage

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:22.789
is an emotion that seems to innately belong
on the Internet.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:28.009
Now, I’m definitely not an angry dude but
I’ve succumbed to my small share of Internet

00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:29.009
rage.

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:34.040
In the dark days following the Game of Thrones
finale, I may have ranted over Gchat to anyone

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.040
who would listen.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.580
I may have even come this close to signing
a toothless Internet petition demanding it

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.660
be remade with better writers.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.010
In short, I raged a little.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.350
And what did I get out of it?

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.350
Not much.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.740
It didn’t make me feel better or more empowered.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.150
I wouldn’t say it was a net benefit for
my life.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.270
But who does benefit when we get pissed off
on the Internet?

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.270
Let’s investigate.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.560
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Internet
rage.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.200
Of course, rage 
on the internet runs the gamut.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.880
It’s generally political, but not always.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.909
It’s sometimes justified, and oftentimes
not.

00:02:04.909 --> 00:02:09.090
Sometimes some celebrity has said something
so blood-boiling you need to watch 5 suggested

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.930
videos about why this person is human trash.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.859
Sometimes, someone gave Sonic baby teeth.

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:20.970
Now, we have a theory about how rage functions
in the online ecosystem, and like most delightful

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.909
and/or vaguely upsetting things in the world,
it’s tied up in money.

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:28.900
Specifically, we’re looking to philosopher
Peter Sloterdijk’s book, Rage and Time:

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:34.290
A Psycho-Political Investigation, which constructs
a history of rage, from Ancient Greece to

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.409
present day.

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:40.620
Sloterdijk starts by arguing that rage is,
at its core, an experience that de-numbs a

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.779
person, a scalding hot coffee mug for the
soul.

00:02:43.779 --> 00:02:49.069
In this way, anger is a fundamentally energizing
force, imbuing you with the vigor necessary

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:51.700
to seize control of your own life.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.190
Like many of our emotions, though, we want
our rage to be recognized or legitimized by

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.329
someone or something more powerful than ourselves.

00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:04.659
Rage, in this sense, is an important aspect
of psychopolitics because it is a demand to

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:05.659
be seen.

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:10.469
Sloterdijk argues, rage ”becomes the momentum
of a movement into the future, which one can

00:03:10.469 --> 00:03:14.489
understand as the raw material for historical
change.”

00:03:14.489 --> 00:03:19.650
Rage starts wars, overthrows governments,
and ends unhappy marriages.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.400
Rage has manifested differently at various
points in human history.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.219
For the ancient Greeks, their Gods sanctioned
their rage by granting them luck in battle.

00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:31.909
But near the end of ancient history, something
shifted.

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:36.359
Collective rage started being seen as a valuable
resource for social manipulation in which

00:03:36.359 --> 00:03:41.829
collective fits of anger were planned into
existence by powerful dudes with highly-specific

00:03:41.829 --> 00:03:42.829
motives.

00:03:42.829 --> 00:03:46.650
Here’s where it’s helpful to start thinking
of rage through an economic lens.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:51.529
Sloterdijk contends that rage should be understood
as an economic resource, of which every person

00:03:51.529 --> 00:03:55.489
has a finite amount that they can spend in
any number of ways.

00:03:55.489 --> 00:03:59.669
Of course, since basically the development
of currency, savvy people have opted not to

00:03:59.669 --> 00:04:05.069
keep their life savings under their mattress
- instead, wisely investing it in banks.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:10.930
Similarly, rage works like an investable monetary
resource you can put in a “rage bank”.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:15.260
The first rage bank, Sloterdijk argues, was
developed by the Catholic church wherein regular

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:20.019
folks were asked to “invest” their anger
through bombastic sermons that threatened

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:21.729
them with eternal damnation.

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:26.280
They accordingly invested all their rage into
the bank of a vengeful God who would come

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.140
back on the Apocalypse and lash out at all
the nonbelievers.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.069
Basically, rather than getting personal revenge
on people who didn’t share their religious

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:40.000
views, believers were assured by the church
that, like a trusty wingman with a sick uppercut,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.990
God would take care of it for them.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.810
Just as a regular bank will hold your money
for you until you need it, so too would the

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.450
Catholic church hold your rage until the Apocalypse.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:54.810
In this way, rage changed the course of history
by creating an effective way for a power structure,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.500
the church, to control the masses.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.220
Sloterdijk finds another palpable manifestation
of economic rage that developed in the late

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.170
19th century with the rise of Communism.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:10.930
The Communist party tapped into the preexisting
anxiety, resentment, and indeed, rage of the

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:16.050
dispossessed poor, telling them that the source
of all their woes lay in the corrupt, brunch-loving

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.199
bourgeoise.

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:22.370
The Communist party very deliberately developed
systems, machinations and operations to produce

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:28.900
rage amongst the lower class, harness it,
and funnel it into the revolution.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:33.750
Through propaganda, manifestos, collective
organizing, and lots and lots of red paraphernalia,

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.750
the Communist party amassed the rage of its
followers, and “invested” it, telling

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.800
them to funnel all their rage into hating
on AND overthrowing the wealthy, and, especially,

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.690
the monarchy.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:51.270
In this way, Sloterdijk sees communist leaders
like Lenin and Mao as the first “rage entrepreneurs,”

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:56.250
finding savvy ways to invest the rage of the
masses into a cause they could all get behind,

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.560
and one which would majorly pay off for those
in charge.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:05.250
Here, we see the way rage literally changed
the course of history by triggering revolution,

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.659
for better or worse.

00:06:06.659 --> 00:06:10.879
In all of the cases above, rage has had somewhere
to live within a culture.

00:06:10.879 --> 00:06:15.980
But Sloterdijk argues, in contemporary society,
there’s nowhere to put it.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:19.740
In the past, communities served as repositories
for anger.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.659
But nowadays, instead of expressing our rage
at the town market or local square dancing

00:06:24.659 --> 00:06:30.620
barn party, we’re hyper-individualistic,
sitting at home, with our airpods in, mumbling

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:34.880
a brilliant, too-late takedown of that guy
who cut you in line at Chipotle.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.669
The point is, solidarity between communities,
religions, and class has faded.

00:06:39.669 --> 00:06:44.190
Consequently, we as a society have failed
to give our collective rage a proper home.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:49.120
Now remember, the crucial part of satisfying
cathartic rage is “recognition” by something

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.370
higher than ourselves.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:56.340
As the world gets hyper connected and individualistic,
meaningful recognition becomes an increasingly

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.030
scarce resource.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:03.360
As a result, a lot more of us feel ignored
or unimportant, and alongside that, sense

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.860
our rage yielding little recognition, creating
a massive group of people who feel exploited

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:10.949
and ignored by those in power.

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:16.040
So what happens to rage when we A. have no
collective place to store it and B. are left

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:20.969
to experience it on our own without any planned
target for it, or any form of validation from

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:21.969
above?

00:07:21.969 --> 00:07:26.419
I’ll give you a hint: What do angry moms,
angry Star Wars fans, angry neo-Nazis, angry

00:07:26.419 --> 00:07:30.860
vegans, angry Beyonce lovers, angry ferret
owners, and angry Christmas stans all have

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:31.919
in common?

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:35.169
They all choose to rage on the Internet!

00:07:35.169 --> 00:07:39.599
The vacuum created by lack of community is
being filled by the internet, allotting rage

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:44.659
to causes ranging from #gamergate to petitions
hating on the Game of Thrones writers.

00:07:44.659 --> 00:07:49.319
Unsurprisingly, studies have shown that high-arousal
emotions, whether positive (like awe at how

00:07:49.319 --> 00:07:54.499
this nebula looks like it’s hosting a rave
for unicorns)or negative (like anger at the

00:07:54.499 --> 00:07:59.699
Ghostbusters remake for ruining your childhood)
are far more likely to go viral than low-arousing

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:05.550
emotions like sadness, a point demonstrated
by viral content mill Upworthy in this graphic.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.219
Rage, in short, is clicky.

00:08:08.219 --> 00:08:12.509
A brief scan of top headlines over the past
few years indicates an almost rabid-level

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:15.919
of anger people are being fed when they check
their newsfeed.

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:21.259
From Obama saluting the Marines while “scandalously”
holding his latte to the new Peter Rabbit

00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:31.939
making light of food allergies, there’s
no shortage of things to be angry about.

00:08:31.939 --> 00:08:37.390
The problem is: in the absence of a rage bank,
there’s nowhere for us to store our anger.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:41.560
Just as you can view the Catholic Church as
a rage bank, and the Communist Party as a

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.400
rage entrepreneur, we think it’s helpful
to contextualize each social media platform

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.290
as a sort of rage “stock exchange,” where
rage is bought, sold, and prospected.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:56.650
Here, let’s go back to Sloterdijk’s explanation
of how any kind of rage economy is formed,

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:01.560
in that it is “planned by its agents according
to entrepreneurial criteria and controlled

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.290
by its managers with long-term oversight.”

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:08.910
In the case of YouTube, the “agents” of
this stock market are the platform owners,

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:14.320
aka Google bigwigs trying to maximize profit,
with the help of algorithms that will maximize

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:15.630
view time.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:22.140
This often means recommending videos that
you will find enraging, because rage = engagement.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:27.600
Individual YouTubers can be seen, like the
leaders of the Communist Party, as rage entrepreneurs,

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.410
directing users’ rage, laser like towards
specific topics that will keep them watching.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:37.430
However, unlike the Communist Revolution,
there is usually not an identifiable “project”

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:41.960
for people to use their rage to actualize;
the only goal on behalf of the entrepreneurs

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:48.100
is self-interest - they’re trying to generate
YouTube views, amass Patreon subscriptions,

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.980
and sometimes, sell apocalypse gift bags.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:55.590
The rage being stirred up is inherently self
perpetuating and fruitless - it’s in the

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:59.740
platform’s interest to keep you raging so
you keep consuming more videos.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:04.480
What’s more, all this YouTubing won’t
help a user amass a coherent dream for a “new

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:08.560
system” because YouTube is always about
to recommend a new, radical thinker with a

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:12.220
newer, radical-er plan to influence you.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.390
As a result, you’re investing all this rage
without any hope of return.

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:20.030
Now, are we telling you to never watch an
angry YouTube video again?

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:21.370
Of course not!

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.480
But we do think it’s important that everybody
recognize the inherent value of their own

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:30.580
rage, both as individuals trying to claim
autonomy over their own lives, and to online

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:33.250
opportunists who want to turn them into loyal
flat-earthers.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:39.780
Remember: there is literally a financial incentive,
both for YouTube and for individual creators,

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:45.180
to keep you angry, and we’re no exception,
which is why I’m taking this moment to inform

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:50.590
you that CleverCrevice doesn’t think Nicolas
Cage deserved an Oscar.

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:52.520
But seriously, what do you guys think?

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:57.770
Is raging on the Internet emotionally healthy,
totally toxic, or just a harmless indulgence

00:10:57.770 --> 00:10:59.850
we all deserve from time to time?

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:04.450
Is the YouTube algorithm making your rage
impotent, and if so, how do you feel about

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:05.450
it?

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:06.450
Let us know in the comments.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.910
Thanks to all our patrons who support the
channel and our podcasts.

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.690
Don’t forget to hit that subscribe button
-

